WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.801
A frequency study answers one question.

2
00:00:04.101 --> 00:00:05.981
Can a new radio site use this

3
00:00:05.981 --> 00:00:09.899
channel without interfering
with anyone licensed nearby?

4
00:00:10.399 --> 00:00:13.179
It finds every nearby licensee,

5
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:17.913
draws their protected areas and the new
site's reach, and checks the overlaps.

6
00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:22.765
A project is thousands of these:
every site, times every channel.

7
00:00:23.165 --> 00:00:30.668
On one project of 7,630 studies, the
web tool it replaced needed 96 minutes.

8
00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:36.607
I rebuilt how studies run, with one rule:
every answer must match the old tool.

9
00:00:37.107 --> 00:00:40.259
Studies now wait in a
queue inside the database.

10
00:00:40.459 --> 00:00:44.861
Each worker claims one and holds a
lease, so any machine can join in.

11
00:00:45.261 --> 00:00:48.342
One shared worker pool
replaced a pool per group.

12
00:00:48.742 --> 00:00:53.685
Searching a state's border for
licensees took 26.6 seconds.

13
00:00:53.985 --> 00:00:57.580
Cut into small pieces, the border
takes a third of a second.

14
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.980
A cloud database would have turned
a ten-minute run into days,

15
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.379
so the engine stays next to its data.

16
00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:10.361
The same 7,630 studies now
finish in under three minutes.

17
00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:13.953
That's 33 times faster,
with the same answers.

18
00:01:14.553 --> 00:01:23.400
In production, one 96-core server ran
151,214 studies in about 40 minutes.
